# Contributing

{{ package_name }} follows the Campbell lab
[Python development standards](https://github.com/campbio/py-dev-standards).
The full rules are in `dev/standards.md` in the repository, and what is
specific to this package is in `AGENTS.md`. They apply to people and to coding
agents alike.

## Setting up

```bash
git clone https://github.com/{{ github_org }}/{{ package_name }}
cd {{ package_name }}
git switch devel
make sync
make check
```

`make help` lists every target. Use the targets rather than calling `pytest`,
`ruff` or `sphinx-build` directly, so everyone runs the same checks with the
same settings.

## Making a change

1. Agree a spec first: what changes, why, and how it is tested. A small change
   needs a few sentences; a larger one gets a file in `dev/plans/`.
2. Branch from `devel`, named `fix/<topic>` or `feature/<topic>`, before writing
   code. Never work on `devel` or `main`.
3. Write a failing test first, then the code that passes it. Check that it fails
   on its assertion, not on an import of something not written yet.
4. Add an entry to `CHANGELOG.md` under `## Unreleased` for anything a user
   would notice, and bump the version in `pyproject.toml`.
5. Add any new public object to `docs/api.md`.
6. Run `make check` as you go and `make check-full` before opening the pull
   request.
7. Open the pull request against `devel`. `main` moves only at a release.

Decisions that are hard to reverse — a new dependency, a change to the public
API, a reorganization of the modules — get an
[architecture decision record](https://github.com/{{ github_org }}/{{ package_name }}/tree/devel/dev/adr)
before the work starts.

## Working with a coding agent

Start the agent from the repository root so it picks up the standards. Claude
Code loads them automatically; other agents are told to read `dev/standards.md`
by `AGENTS.md` and `GEMINI.md`. Review what the agent produces the same way you
would review a colleague's branch: the standards stop before anything is
pushed, and that pause is yours.
